# Canonical-host enforcement, 2026-07-28. The site's own <link rel="canonical">
# (index.html) has always pointed at https://perceptualmechanics.com/, but
# nothing server-side ever enforced it — http://, https://www., and
# /index.html all served the exact same page with no redirect. Google Search
# Console's own "Page indexing" report flagged the result: four URLs bucketed
# under "Alternate page with proper canonical tag" (Scott, 2026-07-28
# screenshot). Not a broken-site bug -- the canonical tag was already doing
# its job, keeping duplicates out of the index -- but relying on it alone
# means any links pointing at a non-canonical variant never consolidate
# their SEO weight onto the real URL, and Google keeps re-crawling
# duplicates instead of just following a redirect once. Real 301s fix both.
#
# Combines the https + apex-domain checks into one rule so http://www...
# takes a single hop straight to the canonical URL, not two.
RewriteEngine On

RewriteCond %{HTTPS} off [OR]
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.perceptualmechanics\.com$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://perceptualmechanics.com/$1 [L,R=301]

# /index.html -> / (THE_REQUEST, not the rewritten path, so this can't loop
# against Vite's own module-script reference to /src/main.js etc.)
RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} \s/+index\.html[?\s]
RewriteRule ^index\.html$ / [L,R=301]

# Same intent, for the static text pages added 2026-07-29: each one is served
# as <dir>/index.html, so /text/scroll/index.html and /text/scroll/ are the
# same page at two URLs — exactly the duplicate the rule above exists to
# collapse. Deliberately kept as its own rule scoped to ^text/ rather than
# generalizing the proven root rule to match any directory: a mistake in an
# untestable Apache regex that only touches /text/ costs a few new pages,
# whereas the same mistake in the root rule could loop the homepage itself.
# %1 is the captured sub-path ('scroll/', or empty for /text/index.html).
RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} \s/+text/(([^\s?]*/)?)index\.html[?\s]
RewriteRule ^text/(.*/)?index\.html$ /text/%1 [L,R=301]

# Cache-Control. Fonts got this treatment 2026-08-31; the hashed bundles and
# HTML joined them 2026-09-01 (4.0), and the two rules that both matched
# .woff2 were reconciled into the one below at the same time. What used to be
# here was a woff2-only `Header set Cache-Control` *plus* a mod_expires
# `ExpiresByType font/woff2` — two directives computing the same header on
# the same response for the same reason. mod_headers wins that race outright
# (`Header set` replaces whatever mod_expires produced), so the mod_expires
# half was doing nothing except waiting for someone to edit one of them and
# not the other. Dropped. Losing its `Expires` header with it costs nothing:
# Cache-Control's max-age takes precedence over Expires everywhere.
#
# js/css — `immutable`, one year. Vite content-hashes these filenames
# (dist/assets/<name>.<hash>.js), so changed content is a different URL by
# construction and a cached copy can never be stale; it is the textbook case
# for `immutable`, which additionally suppresses the revalidation a plain
# max-age still does on reload. DreamHost's own default here was
# max-age=2592000 (30 days, measured in production, no `immutable`). This is
# load-bearing rather than cosmetic: vite.config.js's
# `manualChunks: { three: ['three'] }` exists so a returning visitor keeps
# their cached ~565kB copy of three.js across deploys that don't touch it,
# and that reasoning only pays off if a cache header actually tells the
# browser to keep it.
#
# woff2 — same year, same `immutable`, guaranteed a different way. These are
# the faces that used to come from (and be cached by) Google's CDN; see
# styles/main.css's "Self-hosted fonts" block. They aren't content-hashed,
# but a font swap means a new filename here by this repo's own convention,
# never an overwrite, which gives the same guarantee.
#
# html — the exact opposite, and it has already bitten once. index.html is
# not content-hashed; it is the file that *names* the hashed assets, so a
# cached copy pins a visitor to the previous deploy's entire asset graph.
# DreamHost served max-age=600 (measured), and the first fetch of the
# homepage after the 1.7.0 deploy came back stale, missing a link that a
# `?cb=1` cache-buster returned immediately (NOTES.md, "Verify a deploy
# through a cache-buster"). `max-age=0, must-revalidate` still lets the
# browser keep the copy — it just has to ask first, so an unchanged page
# costs a 304 and a real deploy is never invisible. This also covers the
# eight prerendered text pages, each served as <dir>/index.html.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  <FilesMatch "\.(js|css|woff2)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
  </FilesMatch>
  <FilesMatch "\.html$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# Content Security Policy, 2026-09-01. Shipped Report-Only first (v3.12.0),
# then switched to enforcing here after a fully clean Report-Only pass: all
# ten scenes opened via real pointer clicks, all 11 pmGlimpse hash-allowlisted
# handlers hovered, and Library's video facade + iframe + Open Library cover
# image all exercised — zero policy violations in the console the entire
# time. Enforcing now means a real violation actually blocks the resource,
# not just logs it.
#
# Full external-origin audit before writing this (don't assume, confirm):
# self-hosted fonts left the site with almost no external origins, except
# library.js's YouTube embed/thumbnail and Open Library cover images — both
# real, both allowed explicitly below.
#
# script-src: 'self', and nothing else, as of 4.0. Zero eval()/new Function()
# anywhere in the codebase, and now zero inline script of any kind.
#
# This directive used to carry 'unsafe-hashes' plus eleven 'sha256-' entries,
# one per distinct pmGlimpse('<scene>') string in index.html's
# onmouseover/onfocus attributes — the last inline event handlers left on the
# site, kept working by exact hash rather than by relaxing the directive with
# 'unsafe-inline'. 4.0 retired all 42 of those attributes (21 elements, 11
# distinct strings: ten nav icons, ten preview tiles, #site-title) in favour
# of the delegated addEventListener wiring every other interactive element on
# the site already used. That ended the inline-handler era, so the eleven
# hashes and 'unsafe-hashes' with them are dead allowlist entries — removed
# here in the same release rather than left behind, because an entry nothing
# needs is an entry nobody re-examines, and 'unsafe-hashes' in particular
# widens what a hash match is allowed to be (event-handler attributes, not
# just <script> bodies) for no remaining benefit.
#
# Note for the next reader: STANDARDS.md's "New interactive code always uses
# addEventListener" section describes that eleven-hash list as a closed,
# legacy-only set that stays fixed at eleven. As of 4.0 the set is empty and
# that passage is history, not current state — the rule it states still
# stands, and now has nothing left to grandfather. Adding an on*= attribute
# again would mean re-introducing 'unsafe-hashes' *and* a hash here just to
# keep the site working: friction placed here deliberately.
#
# style-src: 'self' plus exactly one hash, added in 4.0 to fix a shipping bug
# this policy caused. The 3.12.0 audit behind the original 'self'-with-no-
# exceptions line is still accurate as far as it goes: theater.js's reel-hole
# and scroll.js's hand-lettered-jitter effects used to bake Math.random()
# values into style="" attributes (unbounded, not hash-allowlistable) and
# were refactored to apply them via .style.setProperty()/.style.cssText after
# DOM insertion instead — a JS property assignment, which style-src never
# restricts, same category as the WebGL-overlay-positioning pattern used
# elsewhere — and the few static style="" attributes left (colophon.html,
# scroll.html) moved into real CSS rules/classes.
#
# What that audit missed is that this domain serves pages that are not the
# SPA. The eight prerendered pages under /text/ each carry one inline
# <style> and link no stylesheet at all, on purpose: scripts/prerender.js's
# page() builds them to render fully even if every other asset on the domain
# is unreachable. 'self' does not cover an inline block, so from the moment
# this policy went enforcing (3.12.1) until 4.0, the browser dropped it on
# every one of them — document.styleSheets.length === 0, the whole text
# archive served as black-on-white Times at full window width, in production,
# for the entire time. The Report-Only pass that cleared this policy was a
# genuinely thorough audit of the SPA at /; it just never opened a page
# outside it.
#
# The hash below is that block, and it is NOT hand-maintained — do not edit
# it by hand and do not "fix" a mismatch by loosening the directive. `npx
# vite build` re-derives it from the page it has just written and fails the
# build unless three things agree: the emitted /text/index.html, the
# PAGE_STYLE_SHA256 constant declared beside the style text in
# scripts/prerender.js, and this line. Change that CSS, run the build, paste
# the value the error prints. See vite.config.js's verifyStyleHash().
#
# report-to: added 4.0. This policy went enforcing with no report-uri and no
# report-to at all, which is precisely why the style-src bug above sat live
# and unnoticed — a blocked stylesheet is invisible unless someone happens to
# open that exact page with a console open. An enforcing policy with no
# reporting channel only ever tells the one person already looking.
#
# THE ENDPOINT BELOW IS A PLACEHOLDER AND COLLECTS NOTHING TODAY.
# /_csp-reports does not exist on this host and will 404 every POST. That is
# deliberate: a URL that obviously belongs to this site and obviously isn't
# wired up yet is better than pointing real violation reports at a
# third-party collector nobody chose. Before this does anything useful,
# someone has to stand up a real collector — a small endpoint on this host
# that appends to a log file is enough — and put its URL in both places
# below. Until then the directive records the intent and costs one failed
# beacon per violation, which is not nothing but is much less than another
# silent breakage.
#
# Reporting-Endpoints (the response header) and report-to (the CSP
# directive) are a matched pair: both are required and the group name has to
# be identical in each. The deprecated `report-uri` is deliberately not
# mirrored alongside it — it is what Firefox still honours today, but
# duplicating a placeholder into two syntaxes only doubles what has to be
# corrected when the real collector lands. Add it then, if the collector's
# traffic shows it is needed.
#
# frame-ancestors 'none': this site is never meant to be embedded in a frame
# on any page, including its own (Scott's call, 2026-09-01) — only enforceable
# via a real header, not a <meta> tag, hence delivering this here rather than
# in index.html.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'sha256-d0twYSj0NZ/Ct7lC+jDlJkrOgWCQWNuFC2UoM+NHawU='; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: https://i.ytimg.com https://covers.openlibrary.org; connect-src 'self'; frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests; report-to pm-csp"
  Header always set Reporting-Endpoints "pm-csp=\"https://perceptualmechanics.com/_csp-reports\""
</IfModule>

# ─── The other security response headers, 2026-09-01 (4.0) ──────────────────
# Until this release the CSP above was the only security header this file
# set: fetched live, the site sent no Strict-Transport-Security, no
# X-Content-Type-Options, no Referrer-Policy, no Permissions-Policy, no
# X-Frame-Options and no Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy. Each one below is one
# line, and each is written out with its reasoning because the reasoning is
# the part that decays.
#
# `always` on every one of them is load-bearing, not boilerplate. Without
# it, mod_headers only sets a header on a successful (2xx) response and
# drops it on everything else — including the 301s this very file generates
# at the top for http://, www., and /index.html. That is exactly the
# response where HSTS matters most: the plain-http request that gets
# redirected is the one a future visitor should never have to make again.
#
# HSTS starts at 300 seconds ON PURPOSE and is meant to be ramped. Confirm
# nothing on the domain breaks over https-only, then raise it to 31536000
# (one year) — that ramp is the whole reason for the small number, so
# leaving it at 300 forever would be its own kind of failure. Two things are
# deliberately absent and should stay absent unless someone re-derives the
# decision: `preload`, because submitting to the preload list is effectively
# irreversible and bakes the policy into browser binaries; and
# `includeSubDomains`, because this is DreamHost shared hosting and any
# future subdomain that isn't serving HTTPS would simply go dark, with no
# way to reach it to fix it.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff — stop content-type sniffing. Cheap, and
# it matters here specifically because this site serves a lot of static
# files of assorted types out of one document root.
#
# Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin — send the full URL
# same-origin, only the origin cross-origin, and nothing at all when
# downgrading to http. Matches what modern browsers already default to;
# stating it means the site doesn't depend on that default.
#
# Permissions-Policy denies the powerful features this site genuinely never
# uses. fullscreen=(self) is the one exception and it is load-bearing:
# main.js drives the Fullscreen API for the expanded scene view, and
# `fullscreen=()` would silently disable it. Don't tidy that entry away.
#
# X-Frame-Options: DENY is the legacy twin of the CSP's frame-ancestors
# 'none' above. Kept alongside it rather than treated as redundant: the two
# are read by different code paths in some browsers and by a good deal of
# scanning/compliance tooling, and DENY says the same thing frame-ancestors
# already says, so they cannot drift into disagreeing.
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin — this site opens no cross-origin
# windows and wants its own browsing-context group.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT ADDED: Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy. `require-corp` would
# break three things the CSP above explicitly allows on purpose — the
# youtube-nocookie iframe, the i.ytimg.com thumbnails and the
# covers.openlibrary.org cover images in the Library scene — because none of
# those third parties serve CORP/CORS headers for them. Its one real payoff
# is unlocking SharedArrayBuffer and precise timers, which nothing here uses
# or plans to. It is not an oversight; please don't add it as one.
#
# HSTS RAMP — read this before changing the number below.
#
# 300 seconds is a deliberate first value, not a placeholder someone forgot
# to finish. Everything HSTS could break here has already been checked
# against the live site (2026-09-02): http:// has 301'd to https:// site-wide
# since 2026-07-28 and still does, the homepage loads 34 resources with zero
# over http, the certificate is valid, and includeSubDomains is omitted, so
# www and any future subdomain are untouched by this header entirely.
#
# That leaves exactly one thing the short window is guarding, and it is the
# one thing that cannot be tested from anywhere but production: whether
# DreamHost's Apache applies this file at all — mod_headers present,
# .htaccess read, the override allowed. If it does not, the cost of finding
# out is five minutes rather than a year.
#
# The deploy workflow now answers that question by itself. `.github/
# workflows/deploy.yml`'s "Verify live security headers" step fetches the
# live site after every rsync and fails the run if any of these headers is
# missing. So the sequence is: merge, let that step go green once, then
# change the line below to
#
#   Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
#
# and ship it. That is the whole ramp, and it is made on evidence rather
# than on a promise in a comment — which matters, because this project has
# an entry in NOTES.md about exactly what happens to steps a person has to
# remember (sitemap.xml sat stale for months).
#
# Still NOT to be added, at any max-age: `preload`. Submission to the
# browser preload list is effectively irreversible on a human timescale and
# is not undone by lowering or removing this header.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300"
  Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
  Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
  Header always set Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), fullscreen=(self)"
  Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
  Header always set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin"
</IfModule>
